A1. One who always gets ahead in business. ~ MILLINER
A8. A dance step that is pretty much passé. ~ PAS
A11. The Roman way of getting places. ~ LOCI
A12. The way professionals play golf first. ~ RATE
A13. What the family gave Pa for an Xmas present. ~ HINT
A14. Some outside stuff on the race horses. ~ HIDE
A15. Primo Carnero's stiffest punch. ~ ROMAN
A16. A fellow who is really up against it. ~ ANTI
A17. This will give you a straight figure. ~ ONE
A18. Diamond cutters. ~ NINE
A19. Here's when hard-boiled eggs come into their own. ~ EASTER
A21. The way Davis cup players begin sets. ~ SE
A22. This is what opens a man's eyes to a girl's beauty. ~ LID
A23. After you solve this you will have some sense. ~ WIT
A24. The kind of men that can never part with a comb. ~ BALD
A25. A peach preserve. ~ COSMETIC
A30. A paper clip. ~ ITEM
A32. What the woman was who said she never talked about her friends. ~ LIAR
A34. A relief to farmers during the dry spell. ~ ALE
A35. Tacks collector. ~ TIRE
A36. An entertaining guest. ~ EDGAR
A38. This is what movie actresses go around with. ~ FILM
A39. Whoopee juice, extra dry. ~ ADE
A40. The rocky Ford girl. ~ LENA
A41. For lack of space this had to be otherwise. ~ ELSE
A42. The kind of front our best college athletes will put up this year. ~ LETTERED
A46. Scotch corn growers. ~ TAES
A48. This precedes the mode in France. ~ ALA
A49. This puts the finishing touch to your home in Chicago. ~ TNT
A50. This works for the General Electric. ~ GE
A51. A fellow who is a bug on Egyptian things. ~ SCARAB
A54. This has gone over the river. ~ SPAN
A56. Where a man must shift for himself to get ahead. ~ CAR
A57. What the Boston censors did to stories after they read them. ~ TOLD
A58. How the Republicans hope to get power in 1932. ~ AGAIN
A60. What does anyone want of a Judge? ~ COPY
A61. This fights on its stomach. ~ ARMY
A62. What the chimes of a grandfather's clock add to the furnishings of the old homestead. ~ TONE
A63. An Alaskan cape that won't be worn by women this season. ~ NOME
A64. A load off your chest. ~ GAS
A65. Dumb Dora thinks this is a telephone girl. ~ OPERETTA

D1. This is the style that's most modern. ~ MODE
D2. Children make a sucker of this. ~ ICE
D3. A Chinaman would walk a mile for this. ~ LI
D4. The country club that can still make a good highball. ~ IRON
D5. What a man gives his wife at the end of the marriage ceremony. ~ NAME
D6. This is what Dora thought the monster did to the little girl. ~ ETA
D7. What Jimmy Walker does when his vacation is over. ~ RENEWS
D8. Traffic cops use this to wet their whistles. ~ PINT
D9. This makes kitty grow larger. ~ ANTE
D10. The way flappers used to spoon in Grandma's day. ~ STIR
D11. Something at Columbia even One-Eye Connolly could crash. ~ LINE
D13. What the African game hunter makes for a shelter when it grows dark. ~ HASTE
D14. Here is where you will have to be a little patient. ~ HOSPITAL
D15. Something a woman usually is to her husband. ~ RIDDLE
D18. The meaning of the sweet nothings he whispered in her ear. ~ NIL
D20. What Chicago gangsters do to please. ~ AIM
D22. This kind of excuse has no legs. ~ LAME
D24. Here is what Mrs. Coolidge has had on her mind a lot lately. ~ BERET
D25. What Ponzi got for doing the suckers. ~ CAGED
D26. This is all we could squeeze out of that orange. ~ ORAN
D27. This will put an end to all opera coats next season. ~ TAILS
D28. When you get this it will be bed time (plural). ~ ILLS
D29. Eventually this will get all the big stiffs of our generation. ~ CEMETERY
D31. An uprising among the big wets. ~ TIDE
D33. A kind of roomer very unpopular at boarding houses. ~ IDLE
D37. This makes little boys smart in school. ~ RATTAN
D38. A big proposition that the New York police have to handle. ~ FEET
D43. Behind hand. ~ TARDY
D44. This used to be the deuce of a note. ~ ELA
D45. You might find this a little ruff on the neck (var.). ~ RABATO
D47. This makes a short Morrow for Lindbergh. ~ ANN
D50. A confounded expression. ~ GAPE
D51. The debs pet line. ~ STAG
D52. Maiden name, might be Dora's sister. ~ CORA
D53. A free hand out. ~ ALMS
D54. The way all parents of today hope that their children will be minded. ~ SANE
D55. The first place traveling salesmen land when they go to Europe. ~ PIER
D56. The state that senators seem to fancy. ~ COMA
D59. Letters that are most welcome at the White House. ~ GOP
D60. What the soldiers had to fall back on during the war. ~ COT
